# Build context for apps/webapp/Dockerfile ONLY. BuildKit prefers `<Dockerfile>.dockerignore`
# over the root `/.dockerignore`, and the root file is pinned to the organizer's compile
# closure — two images, two closures, two allow-lists (apps/server/Dockerfile.dockerignore
# states the same rule).
#
# Same discipline: DENY EVERYTHING, RE-ADMIT THE BUILD'S INPUTS. This app's closure is the
# packages `next.config.mjs` transpiles from source — ui, tokens, fixtures, client-engine and
# @trafficflow/core (imported only through its dependency-free /ics entry) — plus every
# workspace manifest and the lockfile, because `npm ci` validates the lock against ALL
# workspace manifests it finds. packages/db and packages/db-mail contribute manifests only:
# core declares db as a dependency so npm must be able to LINK it, but nothing in this bundle
# imports it, so its sources stay out.
*

!package.json
!package-lock.json
!apps/*/package.json
!packages/*/package.json

!apps/webapp
!packages/client-engine
!packages/fixtures
!packages/tokens
!packages/ui
!packages/core

# …minus anything inside them that must never be copied in. `.next`/`dist`/`*.tsbuildinfo`
# keep the image compiling from source; `test` is excluded because `next build` type-checks
# every .ts it can see and this app's suite imports siblings that are deliberately NOT in
# this context.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/.next
**/out
**/build
**/test
**/*.tsbuildinfo
**/.DS_Store
**/.claude
**/PRODUCT.md

# Secrets never enter an image layer; every value arrives as a compose/service variable.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.env
